course: dictionary
lesson: 9471
title: Static: still, or a tiny electric zap
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Static has more than one meaning, so use the context to choose the right one. «Rubbing a balloon on dry hair moves tiny electric charges, and each hair pushes away from the others; this is static, and it works best in dry winter air.» Here static means electricity: charges build up and make the hair stand up.
explain: Static can also mean not moving or not changing. A static picture stays still, and prices that stay static do not go up or down. Ask yourself: is something moving here? If not, static probably means still.
explain: Static can also be a noise. A radio with static crackles, and a screen with static has fuzzy dots. Look at the words around it to decide which meaning fits.
explain: «Static shocks from a trampoline can make your hair stand up and give a little zap, but they are harmless.» The word harmless is the clue: the zap does not hurt you, so a static shock is small and safe.
fact: Static electricity is an electric charge that builds up on things when they rub together.
fact: Static electricity can make hair stand up or give you a small shock.
fact: Like charges push apart, and opposite charges pull together.
fact: Lightning is a giant static spark.
fact: «Static» can also mean not moving, as in «static stretches», which are held still.
exercise: «Static shocks from a trampoline can make your hair stand up.» What is static here? | A picture that does not move | A bouncy trampoline | *An electric charge that builds up and gives a small zap | A crackling noise on the radio
exercise: «Prices stayed static all year.» What does static mean? | *Did not change | Went up very fast | Turned into electricity | Made a loud crackle
exercise: «The radio was full of static.» What is the static here? | A swirl of paint | A held still stretch | *A crackling noise | A charge that makes hair stand up
test: «Rubbing a balloon on dry hair moves tiny electric charges.» What does this show about static? | A still picture on a wall | *Electricity that builds up when things rub together | A stretch held for a long time | Fuzzy dots on a screen only
test: Priya says static always means the same as still. Is she right? | No, static can mean electricity or a crackling noise too | *Yes, static only ever means not moving | No, static means very fast | Yes, static means very loud
exam: «Static shocks are harmless, but lightning is a giant static spark.» What does the word static mean in both places? | Fuzzy dots on a screen | Electricity that builds up on things and gives a spark or zap | A still picture | *It means electricity that builds up on things and gives a spark or zap
